Based on my research, the latest MSTest version is **4.2.3**. Here are the breaking changes relevant to your code:

## Breaking Changes for Your Project

### 1. `[ExpectedException]` → `Assert.ThrowsExactly` (MSTEST0006)
In MSTest 4.x, analyzer MSTEST0006 is upgraded to **error severity**, effectively making `[ExpectedException]` obsolete. Replace with `Assert.ThrowsExactly<T>`:

```csharp
// Before
[ExpectedException(typeof(ArgumentNullException))]
[TestMethod]
public void Validate_NullInput_ThrowsArgumentNull()
{
    Validate(null);
}

// After
[TestMethod]
public void Validate_NullInput_ThrowsArgumentNull()
{
    Assert.ThrowsExactly<ArgumentNullException>(() => Validate(null));
}
```

### 2. `Assert.IsTrue(true)` warning (MSTEST0032)
Severity escalates to **Warning** in 4.x. Your `Assert.IsTrue(true)` will trigger a build warning. Replace with a meaningful assertion or remove it.

### 3. Analyzer severity upgrades
Several analyzers that were `Info` in 3.x become `Warning` in 4.x (MSTEST0017, MSTEST0032, etc.), which may cause CI failures if you treat warnings as errors.

## How to Upgrade

Update your `.csproj`:
```xml
<PackageReference Include="MSTest" Version="4.2.3" />
```

Then rewrite your exception tests:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void Validate_NullInput_ThrowsArgumentNull()
{
    Assert.ThrowsExactly<ArgumentNullException>(() => Validate(null));
}

[TestMethod]
public void Process_InvalidState_ThrowsInvalidOperation()
{
    Assert.ThrowsExactly<InvalidOperationException>(() => Process());
}
```

